<article class="env-lemma" id="0FND">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FND">Lemma <span data-tag="0FND">13.30.1</span>.</a> In the situation above assume $F$ is right adjoint to $G$. Let $K \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{D})$ and $M \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ob}}\nolimits (\mathcal{D}')$. If $RF$ is defined at $K$ and $LG$ is defined at $M$, then there is a canonical isomorphism </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{(S')^{-1}\mathcal{D}'}(M, RF(K)) = \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{S^{-1}\mathcal{D}}(LG(M), K)  \]
</div>
  <p> This isomorphism is functorial in both variables on the triangulated subcategories of $S^{-1}\mathcal{D}$ and $(S')^{-1}\mathcal{D}'$ where $RF$ and $LG$ are defined. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $RF$ is defined at $K$, we see that the rule which assigns to an $s : K \to I$ in $S$ the object $F(I)$ is essentially constant as an ind-object of $(S')^{-1}\mathcal{D}'$ with value $RF(K)$. Similarly, the rule which assigns to a $t : P \to M$ in $S'$ the object $G(P)$ is essentially constant as a pro-object of $S^{-1}\mathcal{D}$ with value $LG(M)$. Thus we have 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \begin{align*}  \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{(S')^{-1}\mathcal{D}'}(M, RF(K)) &  = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _{s : K \to I} \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{(S')^{-1}\mathcal{D}'}(M, F(I)) \\ &  = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _{s : K \to I} \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _{t : P \to M} \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{\mathcal{D}'}(P, F(I)) \\ &  = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _{t : P \to M} \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _{s : K \to I} \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{\mathcal{D}'}(P, F(I)) \\ &  = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _{t : P \to M} \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _{s : K \to I} \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{\mathcal{D}}(G(P), I) \\ &  = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _{t : P \to M} \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{S^{-1}\mathcal{D}}(G(P), K) \\ &  = \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _{S^{-1}\mathcal{D}}(LG(M), K) \end{align*}
</div>
    <p>
       The first equality holds by Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/05PY" data-tag="05PY">4.22.9</a>. The second equality holds by the definition of morphisms in $D(\mathcal{B})$, see Categories, Remark <a href="/tag/05Q4" data-tag="05Q4">4.27.15</a>. The third equality holds by Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/002M" data-tag="002M">4.14.10</a>. The fourth equality holds because $F$ and $G$ are adjoint. The fifth equality holds by definition of morphism in $D(\mathcal{A})$, see Categories, Remark <a href="/tag/05Q0" data-tag="05Q0">4.27.7</a>. The sixth equality holds by Categories, Lemma <a href="/tag/05PZ" data-tag="05PZ">4.22.10</a>. We omit the proof of functoriality. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>